WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.215 --> 00:00:07.060
Son presented a very natural approach to
fragment assembly.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:13.310
I, on the other hand, will present a very
unnatural approach to genome assembly.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:17.250
And we will debate later whose approach is
better.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.810
The first thing to notice that in fact,
there

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:25.980
are two Hamiltonian paths in the graph
that Son presented.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.648
This is the first one and this is the
second one.

00:00:29.648 --> 00:00:33.240
And while Son presented his very natural
approach, it's

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.840
absolutely unclear how to find either of
these paths.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.100
I will do something different.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:47.020
First, I will slightly modify the graph.
Remember, in the Hamiltonian path problem,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:52.700
we put 3-mers into nodes

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:58.040
of the graph.
In contrary, I will represent every edge

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:03.150
of my graph as a 3-mer.
See this almost cosmetic difference.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:09.230
It's not clear why do I care so much about
this difference at the moment.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:15.450
If we label the edges of the graph, let's
also try to label nodes of the graph.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.140
And how should we label the nodes of

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.690
the edge TAA?

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:24.160
Let's label the starting node by the
prefix of TAA.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.830
And let's label the ending node by the
suffix of TAA.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:33.310
As a result we will have this graph, shown

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:37.350
below here, it's a path currently not a
graph yet.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:42.740
And this is our starting point, this path
labeled by 3-mers,

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:48.740
where edges are labeled by 3-mers and
nodes are labeled by 2-mers.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:53.340
And now we'll do something really strange
with this graph.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.400
Let's glue identically labeled nodes in
the graph.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.290
In this graph, we have three nodes labeled
AT.

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:03.710
Let's glue them together.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:06.700
Let's bring two of them next to each
other.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:07.710
And next,

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.040
let's bring three of them next to each
other.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.750
Let's bring them even closer to each
other.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:19.890
And finally, they will glue in a single
vertex as shown at this slide.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.240
Now, we haven't done yet, because there

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.600
are more identically labeled nodes on this
graph.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.280
Here it is, three nodes labeled TG.
Let's bring them closer together.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.910
And finally, they will be glued into the
same node.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.050
Are we done?

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.570
Not yet, there are two more nodes that
identically labeled.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:39.680
Those are two nodes GG.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.540
Bring them closer together and glue them
in a single node.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.570
The result is the graph that we will be
study in

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:51.589
this lecture.
It is called De Bruijn Graph of a string.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:58.990
And the question that we first should ask,
how does this graph

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:04.175
can help us to find the genome.
Where is the genome hiding in this graph?

00:03:04.175 --> 00:03:06.020
It isn't hiding at all.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:09.110
Because we started from the genome to
construct this graph.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:12.880
We just made some operations to make the
genome

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.310
somewhat illusive in this graph, but it is
there.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:18.640
It was always there.
Let's take a look.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.040
What corresponds to the genome in this
graph?

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.472
Of course, it is the part that we were
gluing,

00:03:24.472 --> 00:03:28.085
that goes like this.
This, this, this, and this

00:03:28.085 --> 00:03:28.730
path

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.920
along the graph is slowly but surely
builds

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.100
the string, the original string that was
used.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.290
Let's continue doing this.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:37.460
Continue doing this.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:42.535
Well, what are we trying to do?
Well, since we need to explain all

00:03:42.535 --> 00:03:47.800
3-mers in the graph, what we are trying to
do is to find a path

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.922
in the graph that visits every edge 3-mer
exactly once.

00:03:52.922 --> 00:03:55.660
Let's continue.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:59.510
And after we finish this, we construct

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:03.520
actually something that we call an
Eulerian path.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:09.860
The path that visits every edge in the
graph exactly once.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:15.640
And now we have eulerian path problem that
is attributed to Euler, of course.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.640
Find an Eulerian path in the graph which

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.720
is a path visiting every node exactly
once.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.050
And as you remember, Euler solved this
problem

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.450
by trying to solve the Seven Bridges of
Konigsberg problem.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.150
This is an ancient map of Konigsberg.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:38.230
And the orange dot on this map is exactly
where I took you to the field-trip.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:42.800
Now what Euler did, he constructed a graph
by

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.420
connecting four nodes in the graph, each
node corresponds

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.240
to a district of the city.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:52.320
By an edge, if there is a corresponding

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.890
bridge between these two districts in the
city.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:59.960
It is an undirected graph in difference from
directed graphs that we

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.920
mainly consider in this lesson but it
doesn't matter that much.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.240
The differences between directed and
undirected graphs are small,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.650
and we will be mainly working with these
directed graphs.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.600
And we'll let you a chance to

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.570
implement whatever other problems for
for undirected graph.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.540
so we now have two problems.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:24.290
Eulerian path problem and Hamiltonian path
problem.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.020
Do you find many differences here?

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.730
And the question arises, why did I waste

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:35.110
your time reformulating the problems that
Son already presented?

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:37.490
What was my goal?

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:37.720
And in

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:40.360
the next segment, I'll give the answer to
this question

